    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d778">

                <pb facs="0903" n="891" sameAs="frus:frus1942v01/pg891"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811B.001 Quezon, Manuel L./165: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Commanding General of United States Army Forces in
                        the Far East</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">MacArthur</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Chief of Staff</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Marshall</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Fort
                                Mills</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1942-02-02"
                            >February 2, 1942</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>187. In case of ultimate loss of Bataan and consequent siege of Corregidor the
                    question arises as to the ultimate preservation of President Quezon and his
                    family, Vice President Osmeña and the immediate members of the Commonwealth
                    Cabinet. By that time the usefulness of his presence here due to the changed
                    conditions will have been greatly dissipated. I have no means of evacuating him
                    and his physical condition precludes use of air transportation. It is possible
                        <pb facs="0904" n="892" xml:id="pg892"/> he could obtain [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">sustain?</hi>] a submarine trip. Can any plans be
                    arranged from Washington for his possible evacuation thereto? Under the
                    contingency I have described he wishes to take advantage of the previous
                    suggestion that he be evacuated to the United States.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacArthur</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
